WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.197
Welcome to this walkthrough of the Ottodot trial booking project.

2
00:00:04.197 --> 00:00:09.039
This video uses generated English narration and automated screen capture.

3
00:00:09.039 --> 00:00:15.118
The application runs with React, a Hono API, and a persistent SQLite database.

4
00:00:15.118 --> 00:00:19.855
A parent can choose a child, book a trial, and record a mock payment.

5
00:00:19.855 --> 00:00:22.328
Teachers see confirmed learners only.

6
00:00:22.328 --> 00:00:27.315
The central rule is that each trial class has at most four confirmed students.

7
00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:34.024
Starting with Amy and her child Ava, the Space explorers class has seats available.

8
00:00:34.024 --> 00:00:36.340
Book trial opens a pending booking.

9
00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:41.643
At this point, no seat is reserved and Ava is not on the teacher roster.

10
00:00:41.643 --> 00:00:45.630
The simulator makes success and failure easy to demonstrate.

11
00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:50.248
Choosing a failed payment records the attempt and keeps Ava unenrolled.

12
00:00:50.248 --> 00:00:53.814
The interface explains that she can retry the same booking.

13
00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:59.271
A successful retry checks availability again and confirms Ava.

14
00:00:59.271 --> 00:01:04.245
The booking now has one failed attempt and one successful attempt in its history.

15
00:01:04.245 --> 00:01:09.850
Closing the panel and opening the same class returns the existing booking reference.

16
00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:12.179
My bookings provides the same record.

17
00:01:12.179 --> 00:01:20.232
The distinction matters: booking status describes the current enrollment, while payment history preserves what happened during each attempt.

18
00:01:21.374 --> 00:01:24.060
Now for the required last-seat scenario.

19
00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:29.245
Fun with fractions starts with three confirmed children and just one seat available.

20
00:01:29.245 --> 00:01:32.758
Amy starts a booking for Ava but does not complete payment.

21
00:01:32.758 --> 00:01:36.889
This is parent A. Opening checkout does not give A priority.

22
00:01:36.889 --> 00:01:40.205
The displayed availability is only a snapshot.

23
00:01:40.205 --> 00:01:46.060
A different parent can still complete a successful payment and claim that final seat before A does.

24
00:01:47.208 --> 00:01:53.315
In a separate browser session, Ben chooses Noah and starts a booking for the same fractions class.

25
00:01:53.315 --> 00:01:54.697
This is parent B.

26
00:01:54.697 --> 00:01:59.052
Both bookings are pending, but there is still only one available seat.

27
00:01:59.052 --> 00:02:02.197
Ben completes the successful mock payment first.

28
00:02:02.197 --> 00:02:03.855
Noah becomes confirmed.

29
00:02:03.855 --> 00:02:12.908
The server makes this decision inside a database transaction, so the later payment must observe the capacity after Noah has taken the seat.

30
00:02:14.042 --> 00:02:19.728
Returning to Amy, the successful mock payment is now recorded after the class has filled.

31
00:02:19.728 --> 00:02:23.084
Ava receives refund required instead of confirmed.

32
00:02:23.084 --> 00:02:28.689
The message explicitly says that a refund has not been issued and Ava is not enrolled.

33
00:02:28.689 --> 00:02:35.281
Opening the teacher roster shows exactly four children: Eli, Mia, Noah, and Zoe.

34
00:02:35.281 --> 00:02:36.702
Ava is excluded.

35
00:02:36.702 --> 00:02:42.307
Payment success and successful enrollment are separate outcomes when capacity has run out.

36
00:02:43.442 --> 00:02:49.089
Another important failure happens when the server commits but the browser loses the response.

37
00:02:49.089 --> 00:02:54.813
Here, the recording deliberately drops a payment response after the server records success.

38
00:02:54.813 --> 00:02:59.326
The interface keeps the original request key and offers recovery.

39
00:02:59.326 --> 00:03:02.707
Reloading preserves both the booking and that key.

40
00:03:02.707 --> 00:03:08.707
Retrying the same request recovers the confirmed result with one recorded payment attempt.

41
00:03:08.707 --> 00:03:14.194
This avoids guessing whether an uncertain payment should be sent again as a new charge.

42
00:03:15.309 --> 00:03:17.759
The core logic is in recordPayment.

43
00:03:17.759 --> 00:03:23.798
The immediate transaction acquires the database write lock before reading class capacity.

44
00:03:23.798 --> 00:03:30.298
It checks ownership, looks for an existing payment key, and rejects new commands for terminal bookings.

45
00:03:30.298 --> 00:03:36.745
It then checks the class start time and remaining seats, updates the booking, and inserts the payment attempt.

46
00:03:36.745 --> 00:03:39.890
Both writes commit together or roll back together.

47
00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:46.114
SQLite serializes writers, including separate processes using the same database file.

48
00:03:47.219 --> 00:03:49.971
The schema provides a second line of defense.

49
00:03:49.971 --> 00:03:55.497
A unique constraint permits one booking per child and class across all statuses.

50
00:03:55.497 --> 00:03:58.853
Failed payments therefore retry the existing row.

51
00:03:58.853 --> 00:04:00.879
Payment keys are also unique.

52
00:04:00.879 --> 00:04:09.932
Capacity triggers reject a fifth confirmed booking on either insertion or update, even if a future writer bypasses the service.

53
00:04:09.932 --> 00:04:16.866
Foreign keys preserve relationships, and explicit status checks prevent inconsistent payment outcomes.

54
00:04:16.866 --> 00:04:20.905
Confirmed and refund required are terminal in this slice.

55
00:04:22.053 --> 00:04:27.700
An identical payment key replays the original attempt alongside the current booking state.

56
00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:32.516
Reusing that key with another booking or a different outcome returns a conflict.

57
00:04:32.516 --> 00:04:39.595
There is a subtle case worth explaining: an old failed attempt can be replayed after a later successful retry.

58
00:04:39.595 --> 00:04:44.226
The old attempt must stay failed, but the booking must remain confirmed.

59
00:04:44.226 --> 00:04:47.345
The browser displays those two facts separately.

60
00:04:47.345 --> 00:04:53.147
A different key against a terminal booking is refused before another mock payment is recorded.

61
00:04:54.253 --> 00:05:03.663
The repository check passes formatting and lint rules, strict TypeScript checks, thirty tests with ninety nine assertions, and the production build.

62
00:05:03.663 --> 00:05:09.992
The concurrency test starts eight independent Bun processes with separate database connections.

63
00:05:09.992 --> 00:05:12.676
A readiness barrier releases them together.

64
00:05:12.676 --> 00:05:17.295
Exactly one wins the last seat and seven require refunds.

65
00:05:17.295 --> 00:05:22.505
Another test sends five concurrent copies of the same key and records one attempt.

66
00:05:22.505 --> 00:05:31.650
Additional tests cover ownership, invalid input, started classes, database lock errors, persistence, and atomic rollback.

67
00:05:32.763 --> 00:05:37.265
This is a synchronous mock payment command, not a real provider webhook.

68
00:05:37.265 --> 00:05:38.884
No real money moves.

69
00:05:38.884 --> 00:05:48.713
A production payment integration would need verified provider events, amount and currency checks, durable event deduplication, and refund reconciliation.

70
00:05:48.713 --> 00:05:52.068
Network calls must stay outside the database lock.

71
00:05:52.068 --> 00:05:58.160
Temporary holds or authorization before capture could improve the last-seat experience.

72
00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:03.265
At higher write volume, PostgreSQL could lock individual class rows.

73
00:06:03.265 --> 00:06:09.555
Authentication, cancellation, and refund execution remain outside this take-home slice.

74
00:06:10.673 --> 00:06:13.333
The demo is also available on Cloudflare.

75
00:06:13.333 --> 00:06:22.123
A Worker serves the frontend and routes API requests to one SQLite Durable Object shared by every parent and class.

76
00:06:22.123 --> 00:06:25.557
It runs the same booking service and schema shown here.

77
00:06:25.557 --> 00:06:32.820
Locally, Bun uses an immediate transaction; Cloudflare uses its native synchronous transaction API.

78
00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:35.438
Bookings persist across redeployments.

79
00:06:35.438 --> 00:06:44.373
Most implementation code was generated with Codex, alongside tests, documentation, illustrations, and this narrated walkthrough.

80
00:06:44.373 --> 00:06:49.991
The AI usage file records those contributions and the corrections made during review.

81
00:06:49.991 --> 00:06:58.675
The completed solution demonstrates safe last-seat allocation, recoverable payment requests, and a roster drawn from confirmed bookings.

82
00:06:58.675 --> 00:07:05.715
The public repository includes setup instructions and test evidence, and the live app is linked in the README.

83
00:07:05.715 --> 00:07:06.965
Thank you for watching.
